import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Screenshot from '../../components/Screenshot.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="Files and assets" activeNav="/platform/files-and-assets">
  <h1>Files and assets</h1>
  <p>
    An application's media library holds two different things, separated into tabs:
    <strong>Files</strong> are arbitrary attachments with metadata — mapping files, release
    checklists, provisioning notes. <strong>App assets</strong> are the icon and splash screen,
    from which the platform generates every per-platform size you need at build time.
  </p>

  <h2>Files</h2>
  <Screenshot
    src="files-and-assets"
    alt="Files tab showing two attached files as cards with file type icons, sizes, and action buttons"
    caption="Each file card shows the type preview, name, size, upload date, and action buttons."
  />
  <p>
    Any file type, up to 100 MB each. Each card shows a preview (image thumbnail, video player,
    or a themed icon for documents, archives, and other types), the file name, size, and upload
    time. Below the preview, three actions are available: inspect <strong>Metadata</strong>,
    download, and delete.
  </p>
  <p>
    Files are addressed <strong>by name</strong>, not by id — in the URL, in the API, and in the
    CLI. Two consequences follow:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Uploading a name that already exists does not overwrite the existing file. The platform
      de-duplicates by giving the new upload a distinct name. Delete the old one first if you meant
      to replace it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Names cannot contain slashes. That restriction is what keeps a name from escaping the app's
      own collection.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Uploading a file</h3>
  <Screenshot
    src="upload-file-modal"
    alt="Upload file modal with file picker and optional metadata JSON textarea"
    caption="Metadata can be set during upload or added later from the file card."
  />
  <p>
    The upload modal accepts any file type. An optional <strong>Metadata</strong> field lets you
    attach a JSON object at upload time — for example to record the build that produced the file
    or the version it belongs to.
  </p>

  <h3>Metadata</h3>
  <p>
    Metadata is a free-form JSON object attached to a file. It is the practical way to record where
    a file came from, and it is what makes an old file identifiable a year later.
  </p>
  <Screenshot
    src="files-metadata"
    alt="File card with metadata preview open showing formatted JSON and an edit button"
    caption="Click Metadata on any file card to inspect or edit its JSON payload."
  />
  <p>
    Click the <strong>Metadata</strong> button on a file card to toggle the metadata preview. The
    preview shows formatted JSON. Click the pencil icon to enter edit mode, change the JSON, and
    save. The value must be a JSON object (not an array, null, or a primitive).
  </p>
  <p>
    Saving replaces the whole object rather than merging into it, so send every key you want to
    keep. Example:
  </p>
  <pre><code>&#123;"build_id": "019fc789-47b5-7034-9291-4cd3c57b75d5", "version": "1.4.0"&#125;</code></pre>
  <p>
    Through the CLI, metadata is set with
    <a href="/cli/files"><code>appmctl files update --metadata</code></a>.
  </p>

  <h3>What belongs here, and what does not</h3>
  <p>
    Files belong to the <em>application</em> and outlive any single build. Build outputs belong to
    the build that produced them, as <a href="/platform/builds">artifacts</a>, and disappear with
    it. The usual split in a pipeline: the AAB or IPA goes to artifacts, the symbol mapping goes
    here, where it stays findable after the build ages out.
  </p>

  <h2>App assets</h2>
  <Screenshot
    src="app-assets"
    alt="App assets tab showing the icon collection with source preview and generated platform variants"
    caption="One source image produces every iOS and Android size automatically."
  />
  <p>
    The <strong>App assets</strong> tab holds two static collections: an <strong>icon</strong> and a
    <strong>splash screen</strong>. Unlike files, assets are <strong>images only</strong> (PNG,
    JPEG, or WEBP), have <strong>no metadata</strong>, and trigger automatic processing on upload.
  </p>
  <p>
    The detail view is split into a source preview on the left and a grid of generated variants on
    the right, grouped by platform:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Asset</th><th>iOS variants</th><th>Android variants</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>App icon</td><td>11 sizes (40 px to 1024 px)</td><td>6 densities (mdpi to xxxhdpi) + Play Store icon</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Splash screen</td><td>12 sizes (640×1136 to 2048×2732)</td><td>5 densities (320×480 to 1280×1920)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Uploading replaces the previous source — there is no version history. Generation runs after
    upload rather than during it, so a variant list can briefly show fewer entries than it will a
    moment later. A variant that never appears has not been generated, and requesting it answers
    <code>404</code> rather than silently falling back to the original.
  </p>
  <p>
    Replacing an icon also triggers an Expo sync when the app is linked to an Expo project, so the
    two sides do not drift — see <a href="/platform/integrations/eas">EAS</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Key differences: Files vs. App assets</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th>Files</th><th>App assets</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Content types</td><td>Any file type</td><td>Image only (PNG, JPEG, WEBP)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Metadata</td><td>Free-form JSON, user-editable</td><td>None</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Processing</td><td>Stored as-is</td><td>Auto-generates platform-specific sizes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Upload</td><td>Modal with file picker + optional JSON</td><td>Inline file input, no metadata</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Delete</td><td>Per-file with confirmation</td><td>Source can only be replaced, not deleted</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Variants</td><td>N/A</td><td>Per-platform grid with status and download</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Doing the same from the CLI</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="/cli/files"><code>appmctl files</code></a> — list, inspect, download, upload,
      update metadata, delete
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/cli/applications"><code>appmctl assets</code></a> — list every generated
      variant with its download URL, then fetch one
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <code>appmctl assets list</code> is the quickest way to see which variants actually exist for an
    app, which is more reliable than assuming a size was generated.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
